When we think of Health & Safety, it’s easy to focus on compliance checklists and regulatory obligations. But in this insightful guest blog from our trusted partners at [Marah Associates](https://www.marahassociates.com/), we’re reminded that the real heart of any effective H&S strategy is your people.

Our Founder Roz, previously used to work with our guest blogger, Sarah, so we know firsthand the depth of their expertise and their practical, people-first approach.

Drawing on their experience supporting businesses across the UK and Europe, Marah shares actionable strategies to help you go beyond box-ticking and build a culture that truly values safety, accountability, and care.

If you’re ready to strengthen your workplace culture, reduce risk, and put your people at the centre of your safety strategy, this one’s for you!

When we talk about Health & Safety, it’s easy for conversations to drift toward compliance, policies, risk assessments, and regulations. While these are essential components, they are only part of the picture. At the heart of every robust Health & Safety (H&S) strategy is one simple principle: *people come first.*

As a consultancy partnering with businesses across the UK and Europe, we’ve seen firsthand that the most successful organisations don’t treat health and safety as a tick-box exercise. They make it a cultural priority, one that empowers their people, protects their operations, and strengthens their reputation.

So, how can your business start prioritising people in Health & Safety?

## **1. Listen First, Lead Second**

The best H&S strategies are informed by the people on the ground. Whether it’s production staff, contractors, or managers, your team has valuable insights into daily risks and near-miss situations. Make time for regular feedback, formal or informal, and act on it.

**Tip:**
Introduce a "You Said, We Did" approach to show staff that their voices are being heard and acted upon. Engagement leads to ownership, and ownership drives safer behaviours.

## **2. Train with Purpose, Not Just Policy**

Annual e-learning courses might tick a box, but real impact comes from targeted, hands-on, and engaging training. People need to understand *why* safety matters—not just what the rules are.

**Tip:**
Mix up your training methods - toolbox talks, visual demonstrations, on the job coaching and tailor the content to the specific risks your teams encounter.

## **3. Make Safety Personal**

When safety is personal, it becomes powerful. Remind teams that H&S isn't just about avoiding fines or following rules, it's about getting home safe every day to their families, friends, and futures.

**Tip:**
Share stories - not just statistics. Real life examples resonate far more than numbers. Celebrate ‘good catches’, safety heroes, and positive outcomes.

## **4. Empower Your Leaders**

Supervisors, team leaders, and managers play a vital role in setting the tone for safety. Equip them with the confidence and competence to spot risks, challenge unsafe behaviour, and model best practice.

**Tip:**
Invest in leadership development focused on safety culture—not just compliance. A strong safety culture is built from the top down and the bottom up.

## **5. Partner for Progress**

Not every business has the internal capacity to keep up with the evolving landscape of legislation, training needs, and industry-specific risks. That’s where partnering with an experienced H&S consultancy can make all the difference.

We work alongside businesses to:

- Review and refresh their current safety management systems

- Build practical and realistic action plans and roadmaps

- Embed a culture of accountability and care

- Provide training, support, and an extra pair of eyes when needed most

- Provide expertise across sectors

- Provide a Competent Person

## **The Bottom Line**

Prioritising people in Health & Safety isn’t just the *right* thing to do - it’s a smart business decision. It reduces downtime, boosts morale, improves retention, and enhances reputation. But most importantly, it creates a working environment where your people feel safe, valued, and heard.

